WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:08.000
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.600
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

3
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:23.380
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

4
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:31.340
to stop killing each other" tax): VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bond investors receive ten percent, or two point seven two

5
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:40.480
billion dollars, to repay campaign funders. Political incentives (I.A.B.s) receive ten percent, or two point seven two billion

6
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:49.160
dollars, to keep politicians aligned. Eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, is available for Wishocracy,

7
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:56.580
which is what you get to allocate. How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight

8
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:05.680
billion dollars? If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists

9
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.600
the way a dropped ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

10
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.680
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

11
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:24.480
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

12
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.440
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

13
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.560
is like asking two hundred bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

14
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.650
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

15
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.860
How Wishocracy Allocates the one percent Treaty Fund: Decentralized Crowdfunding.

16
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.920
Your decentralized institutes of health (D.I.H.) network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

17
00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.420
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates funds from the one percent Treaty Fund across competing campaigns.

18
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:07.100
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

19
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.300
What the decentralized framework for drug assessment (D.F.D.A.

20
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:20.040
(a decentralized F.D.A.)) handles automatically (no voting required): Which treatments get tested: Companies register, patients

21
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:28.080
choose (capitalism, for once, doing something useful). Which diseases get researched: Patients join trials for their conditions

22
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:36.100
(people tend to be motivated about their own mortality). Resource allocation within trials: Market prices and participant

23
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:45.460
choices. What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent

24
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:50.940
Treaty Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

25
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:57.460
The Decentralized Framework for Drug Assessment Development is a one billion dollars per year proposal.

26
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.980
The Epic E.H.R. Integration Project is five million dollars one-time.

27
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.520
A.W.S. Infrastructure Services is three million dollars per year.

28
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.360
The Security Audit Program is two million dollars per year.

29
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.840
An alternative decentralized framework for drug assessment is an eight million dollars per year proposal (competition!

30
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:24.860
novel concept for government!). Public Goods (things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that

31
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:31.920
don't sell pills). The Patient Trial Subsidies Program receives eight hundred million dollars per year through an automatic

32
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.700
formula. Off-Patent Drug Research receives twenty billion dollars per year.

33
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.280
The Rare Disease Initiative receives ten billion dollars per year.

34
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:50.500
The Negative Results Publishing Fund receives five billion dollars per year (yes, knowing what doesn't work is worth billions;

35
00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.080
your species keeps re-running failed experiments because nobody publishes failures).

36
00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:03.580
Service Provider Bids (capitalism, but supervised). Data Storage Provider A receives two billion dollars per year.

37
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.940
Data Storage Provider B receives one point five billion dollars per year (competing).

38
00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.860
The Pragmatic Clinical Trial Insurance Pool has a ten billion dollar reserve.

39
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:22.900
How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns. No committee on Earth has ever looked at a list of ten thousand proposals

40
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.300
and made a good decision. Committees are where good ideas go to die of old age.

41
00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:36.200
Instead, the global population votes using pairwise comparisons: "What's more important right now:" Framework for drug assessment

42
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.420
development versus Alzheimer's prize? Epic integration versus Security audits?

43
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.440
Rare disease research versus An alternative drug assessment framework?

44
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:53.480
Patient subsidies versus Infrastructure spending? Millions of people make simple pairwise choices.

45
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.320
The algorithm aggregates preferences into funding allocations.

46
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:05.020
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

47
00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:11.480
Why You Can't Just Use a Spreadsheet. Someone is going to ask, "Why not just write a formula?"

48
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.420
Because the decisions look like this: One. Multiple competing implementations.

49
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.460
Three teams want to build the drug assessment platform. Which one?

50
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:28.020
All three? Two? The one with the best logo? Two. Trade-offs between prizes.

51
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:36.100
One hundred billion dollars for aging reversal or fifty billion dollars for Alzheimer's plus fifty billion dollars for cancer?

52
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:44.640
There is no "correct" answer. There is only what humanity collectively values, which is exactly what Wishocracy measures.

53
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.480
Three. Public goods allocation. How much for off-patent drugs versus rare diseases?

54
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.040
Markets won't answer this because there's no profit in it. Four.

55
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.100
Service provider competition. Which companies get contracts for what amounts?

56
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.220
Your decentralized framework for drug assessment, or D.F.D.A.

57
00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.360
(a decentralized F.D.A.), allocates resources WITHIN pragmatic clinical trials.

58
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.680
Wishocracy allocates resources BETWEEN campaigns. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

59
00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:26.540
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. The core of Wishocracy is a voting method so simple, even a congressman

60
00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:34.200
could do it (though we'd recommend they practice first). It's called Aggregated Pairwise Preference Allocation, or simply,

61
00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:39.420
the Pairwise Slider Allocation. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

62
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.440
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

63
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:53.220
Instead of asking, "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now: D.F.D.A.

64
00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.990
platform development or security audits?" You make a choice.

65
00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.220
That's it. You do this a few times with different random pairs.

66
00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:07.880
It takes five minutes. Less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show, and considerably more important.

67
00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:15.000
Millions of other people do the same. An algorithm takes all these simple, head-to-head comparisons and builds a real-time

68
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:22.040
ranking of infrastructure and public goods priorities. (Remember: Wishocracy doesn't allocate between diseases.

69
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:29.540
Patients do that by choosing which trials to join. Wishocracy allocates between infrastructure campaigns and public goods

70
00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:35.860
that markets can't handle.). It's the wisdom of crowds, but without the crowds having to talk to each other.

71
00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:44.340
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning). When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical happens

72
00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:51.660
(and by "magical" I mean "statistically inevitable," which is the only kind of magic that actually works): Random pairs

73
00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:56.880
prevent gaming. You can't lobby 5 million people. Well, you can try.

74
00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:04.800
Good luck. Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons.

75
00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.520
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's medical priorities.

76
00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.870
Google uses it to find cat videos. You'll use it to cure cancer.

77
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:23.580
Progress. Outliers cancel out. The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack" gets drowned out by the millions voting for

78
00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:28.740
"cure my mother's Alzheimer's." Wisdom of crowds emerges. Reliably.

79
00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:34.860
Repeatedly. Boringly. Example with real numbers: 5 million people vote.

80
00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:42.210
Each makes 20 comparisons. That's 100 million data points. Algorithm processes them in seconds.

81
00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:49.180
Output: "Allocate forty percent to patient subsidy programs, twenty-five percent to D.F.D.A.

82
00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:56.920
(a decentralized F.D.A.) platform development, fifteen percent to off-patent drug research, ten percent to rare disease

83
00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:03.500
initiatives, five percent to security infrastructure, five percent to negative results publishing..."

84
00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:06.040
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

85
00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:14.080
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

86
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.040
and thus cancels out. This is called "the wisdom of crowds."

87
00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:25.200
It works for the same reason that guessing the weight of a cow at a fair works: individually you're all terrible at it,

88
00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:31.780
but collectively you're weirdly accurate. From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks).

89
00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:38.640
Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's" is a top priority.

90
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:43.540
Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

91
00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.980
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

92
00:09:50.700 --> 00:10:00.180
A.I. Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller, concrete,

93
00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:05.660
fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

94
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:12.980
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

95
00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:19.660
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

96
00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:27.040
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it

97
00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:33.820
to medicine. The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

98
00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:39.720
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

99
00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:47.180
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

100
00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:56.040
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

101
00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:03.940
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

102
00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.680
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

103
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:16.600
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

104
00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:20.260
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.
